# (id,name,latin,synonyms,kind('v' vessel / 'n' nerve),region,location,description,function,clinical)
SUP=[
('axillary-artery','Axillary artery','Arteria axillaris',[],'v','upper-limb','Armpit (axilla), from the outer border of the first rib to the lower border of teres major.','The continuation of the subclavian artery. It lies behind pectoralis minor and is wrapped by the cords of the brachial plexus.','Carries oxygen-rich blood to the shoulder, chest wall and arm. It becomes the brachial artery.','A shoulder dislocation or a fracture of the surgical neck of the humerus can injure it and the axillary nerve. Its branches link around the shoulder blade, so blood can still reach the arm if the main vessel is blocked slowly.'),
('radial-artery','Radial artery','Arteria radialis',[],'v','upper-limb','Outer (thumb) side of the front of the forearm, then the wrist and the back of the hand.','One of the two end branches of the brachial artery. It runs under brachioradialis and lies just under the skin at the wrist.','Supplies the outer forearm, the thumb side of the hand and (with the ulnar artery) the deep palmar arch.','The pulse is felt here at the wrist. It is the usual site for arterial blood gas samples and for a catheter in heart procedures. Before it is used, the Allen test shows that the ulnar artery can supply the hand.'),
('ulnar-artery','Ulnar artery','Arteria ulnaris',[],'v','upper-limb','Inner (little finger) side of the front of the forearm, then the palm.','The larger end branch of the brachial artery. It runs deep to the flexor muscles, then beside the ulnar nerve to the wrist and forms the superficial palmar arch.','Supplies the inner forearm and most of the palm and fingers through the superficial palmar arch.','A cut at the wrist often injures the ulnar artery together with the ulnar nerve and the tendons. Damage to the palmar arch (for example in a deep cut of the palm) causes heavy bleeding that must be controlled from both the radial and ulnar sides.'),
('popliteal-artery','Popliteal artery','Arteria poplitea',[],'v','lower-limb','Back of the knee (popliteal fossa).','The continuation of the femoral artery after it passes through the adductor hiatus. It lies deepest in the fossa, next to the femur and the knee capsule, and divides below the knee.','Supplies the knee joint and the muscles around it, then divides into the anterior and posterior tibial arteries.','A knee dislocation or a fracture of the lower femur can tear or block it, which threatens the leg within hours. A popliteal aneurysm is the commonest peripheral aneurysm and is found as a throbbing swelling behind the knee. The pulse is felt with the knee bent.'),
('anterior-tibial-artery','Anterior tibial artery','Arteria tibialis anterior',[],'v','lower-limb','Front of the leg, on the interosseous membrane between the tibia and fibula.','Arises below the knee, passes forward through the top of the interosseous membrane and runs down the front of the leg with the deep fibular nerve. At the ankle it becomes the dorsalis pedis artery.','Supplies the muscles of the front of the leg (the extensor compartment) and the top of the foot.','The dorsalis pedis pulse on the top of the foot is checked to assess blood flow in peripheral arterial disease. Swelling in the front compartment of the leg (compartment syndrome) can squeeze this artery and the deep fibular nerve and cause foot drop.'),
('posterior-tibial-artery','Posterior tibial artery','Arteria tibialis posterior',[],'v','lower-limb','Back of the leg, deep to soleus, then behind the inner ankle bone.','The larger end branch of the popliteal artery. It runs with the tibial nerve down the back of the leg and divides into the medial and lateral plantar arteries in the sole.','Supplies the calf muscles and the sole of the foot. It gives off the fibular (peroneal) artery.','The pulse is felt behind the medial malleolus. Absent pulses with cold, painful feet suggest peripheral arterial disease; this is common in people who smoke or have diabetes.'),
('axillary-vein','Axillary vein','Vena axillaris',[],'v','upper-limb','Armpit, in front of and below the axillary artery.','Formed where the basilic vein joins the brachial veins. It becomes the subclavian vein at the outer border of the first rib.','Drains blood from the arm and the upper chest wall back towards the heart.','Deep vein thrombosis here (for example after a pacemaker wire or a central line, or from hard repeated arm effort) causes a swollen, bluish arm.'),
('cephalic-vein','Cephalic vein','Vena cephalica',[],'v','upper-limb','Just under the skin on the thumb (outer) side of the forearm and the outer side of the upper arm.','A long superficial vein that runs in the groove between deltoid and pectoralis major and drains into the axillary vein.','Drains the outer side of the hand, forearm and arm.','Often used for drips and blood tests, and for a fistula for kidney dialysis. Cutting down on it at the shoulder gives access to the venous system.'),
('basilic-vein','Basilic vein','Vena basilica',[],'v','upper-limb','Just under the skin on the little finger (inner) side of the forearm. In the arm it dives deep to join the brachial veins.','A long superficial vein on the inner side of the arm. Above the elbow it passes through the deep fascia to form the axillary vein with the brachial veins.','Drains the inner side of the hand and forearm.','A common site for drawing blood and placing a PICC line. The median cubital vein, which links it to the cephalic vein at the elbow, is the usual place for venepuncture; the median nerve and brachial artery lie deep to it.'),
('femoral-vein','Femoral vein','Vena femoralis',[],'v','lower-limb','Front and inner side of the thigh, beside the femoral artery.','The continuation of the popliteal vein. It lies on the inner side of the femoral artery in the femoral triangle and becomes the external iliac vein behind the inguinal ligament.','Drains blood from the deep structures of the leg and thigh.','A common site of deep vein thrombosis, which causes a swollen, painful leg and may send a clot to the lungs (pulmonary embolism). It is the vein used for access in many heart and venous procedures.'),
('great-saphenous-vein','Great saphenous vein','Vena saphena magna',['long saphenous vein'],'v','lower-limb','Just under the skin along the inner side of the leg and thigh, from the inner ankle to the groin.','The longest vein in the body. It starts at the inner side of the foot, passes in front of the medial malleolus, ascends the inner leg and thigh and joins the femoral vein at the saphenous opening.','Drains the skin and superficial tissues of the leg. It lies near the saphenous nerve.','Varicose veins form when its valves fail. Segments are harvested for coronary bypass grafts. A cut-down at the ankle gives emergency venous access.'),
('median-nerve','Median nerve','Nervus medianus',[],'n','upper-limb','Runs down the middle of the front of the arm and forearm, then through the carpal tunnel into the palm.','Formed from the lateral and medial cords of the brachial plexus (C5 to T1). It lies beside the brachial artery in the arm, passes between the two heads of pronator teres and runs between the superficial and deep finger flexors.','Supplies most of the forearm flexors, the thenar muscles (thumb) and the first two lumbricals. Gives sensation to the thumb, index, middle and half of the ring finger on the palm side.','Carpal tunnel syndrome (compression under the flexor retinaculum) causes numbness and tingling of the thumb, index and middle fingers and weak thumb movement. A high lesion gives the "hand of benediction" when the person tries to make a fist. It can be injured in supracondylar fractures of the humerus.'),
('ulnar-nerve','Ulnar nerve','Nervus ulnaris',['funny bone nerve'],'n','upper-limb','Inner side of the arm, behind the medial epicondyle (the funny bone), then along the inner forearm to the little finger side of the hand.','A branch of the medial cord of the brachial plexus (C8, T1). It passes behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus, between the two heads of flexor carpi ulnaris, and enters the hand beside the pisiform.','Supplies flexor carpi ulnaris, half of flexor digitorum profundus, most small hand muscles and the skin of the little finger and half of the ring finger.','Hitting the elbow ("funny bone") gives tingling in the little finger. Cubital tunnel syndrome and injury cause a claw hand, weak grip and loss of finger spreading (Froment sign).'),
('saphenous-nerve','Saphenous nerve','Nervus saphenus',[],'n','lower-limb','Inner side of the thigh, knee, leg and foot, with the great saphenous vein.','The longest branch of the femoral nerve (L3, L4). It runs in the adductor canal and then beside the great saphenous vein.','Gives sensation to the skin of the inner side of the leg and foot.','It can be injured in saphenous vein surgery or knee operations and cause numbness or burning along the inner leg. It is blocked in the adductor canal for knee surgery pain relief.'),
]
